          Toro sells electric snow throwers in four distinct classes, and the mistake most buyers make is comparing them on price alone. A corded Power Shovel and a 60V two-stage share a logo and almost nothing else. Pick the class from your snow and your driveway first, then the model, and the decision gets short.
        </p>

        <TopPick />

        <div id="the-lineup" class="scroll-mt-24">
          <h2 class="text-3xl font-black text-gray-900 mt-16 mb-6">
            1. Toro's Electric Lineup at a Glance
          </h2>

          <p>
            Toro's electric snow throwers fall into four groups, and each group answers a different question. The corded Power Shovel is for steps, decks and short walks. The corded Power Curve is a lightweight single-stage for a small, paved driveway within reach of an outlet. The 60V Flex-Force single-stage machines take that same job cordless and stretch it to a bigger driveway. And the 60V Power Max e24 is a battery two-stage, the one electric Toro that is meant to stand in for a gas machine in real snow country.
          </p>

          <p>
            That spread matters because the failure mode of each class is different. A shovel that is asked to clear a two-car driveway is not broken, it is just the wrong tool, and it will take an hour. A single-stage that meets a foot of wet plow ridge stalls and pushes rather than throws. A two-stage that is bought for an eight-foot walk is a heavy, expensive machine sitting in the garage. Matching the class to the job is ninety percent of the decision, and the model number inside the class is the last ten percent.
          </p>

          <p>
            Toro also refreshes model numbers and kit bundles from season to season, often changing only the battery that ships in the box. That is why this guide describes the lineup by class rather than pretending one SKU is the only option. When you shop, the clearing width, the stage count, the battery voltage and the included battery capacity tell you almost everything. If a listing does not state those four things plainly, check Toro's own product page before you buy.
          </p>

          <p>
            If you already know you want the strongest battery machine in the category, our <a href="/best-electric-snow-blower/">ranked list of the best electric snow blowers</a> puts the Power Max e24 against EGO, Greenworks, Ryobi and Snow Joe. If you are still deciding between battery and gas at all, start with <a href="/electric-vs-gas-snow-blower/">electric vs gas snow blowers</a>, because that choice narrows the field faster than anything in this guide.
          </p>

